            <ArticleTitle>14-year-old boy with painful nail changes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e8-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1542/pir.36-3-e8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Doxycycline is a classically implicated cause of phototoxic reactions. Photo-onycholysis is a rare manifestation of phototoxicity. Nail changes may typically include nail pain, subungual hemorrhages, or distal onycholysis. Self-resolution can be expected in 3 to 6 months. Patients prescribed doxycycline should be counseled about the potential for phototoxicity and the need for appropriate sun protection while taking the medication.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
